## std.argv — command-line argument access.
##
## From docs/spec.md §6.4 (System Surfaces): argv is exposed as a
## free-fn form after the user writes `pub import std.argv.{get}`.
##
## v0.5 self-hosting migration: REAL pure-zag impl. The previous
## binding was PREAMBLE-side: `stdlibPreambleName("get")` returned
## `__zag_argv` so the imports loop's fast path emitted
## `const get = __zag_argv;` IN THE USER MODULE — the @import slow
## path could not work because the materialized std/argv.zig was a
## separate zig module whose own preamble copy of `__zag_argv` was
## never assigned (only the user module's global was captured at
## main entry by genFun's is_main special case). Mirrored the
## String/Writer hardcoded-coupling rationale.
##
## That whole apparatus retires now: lib/std/posix.zag's argv()
## reads /proc/self/cmdline directly (kernel-provided, identical
## NUL-separated encoding to the /proc/self/environ walk getenv
## already does), so no main-entry capture, no `init` threading,
## and no preamble global are needed. This module is a plain
## delegation through the standard @import+alias fallthrough —
## exactly the shape process.zag's exit/exec use. Reading the
## `__zag_argv` name anywhere is the regression signal.
##
## The slice is the actual argv (argv[0] included, bounded by the
## kernel's own layout); elements are borrowed from posix.zag's
## argv_buf — the next argv() call overwrites them, matching the
## getcwd/getenv contract. `get()` takes no arguments: the old
## per-call blk-walker form took an optional index, but the
## docs/spec.md §6.4 surface and every call site use the bare
## whole-vector form (`let args = get(); args[i]`).

pub import std.posix.{argv as sys_argv}

pub fun get() -> []const []const u8 {
    return sys_argv();
}
